# Ship-loop terminal states

Ordinary pushed-but-unmerged work is still unfinished. Do not weaken the normal
commit -> push -> PR -> CI -> merge -> live contract and do not treat
`merge-on-green` by itself as completion.

There is one distinct operator-controlled terminal state: **PARKED**.

A pull request is PARKED only when all of these are true:

- its title starts with `HOLD-FOR-SOL`;
- it is still a draft;
- its exact current head is pushed and the worktree is clean;
- it has no `merge-on-green` label and native GitHub auto-merge is not armed;
- the PR body or comments record an explicit Sol authority and a release condition
  controlled by Sol;
- the hold explicitly says not to merge;
- all binding checks on that exact head have concluded clean.

When those conditions hold, the unmerged state is intentional and externally
controlled. Report the work as **PARKED / HOLD-FOR-SOL**, not SHIPPED and not
`SHIP LOOP BLOCKED`. Do not merge, mark ready, arm automation, relabel, or repeatedly
poll/retry merely to satisfy the ordinary merge completion rule. End the session
after one concise evidence report. Resume the ship loop only after Sol releases or
changes the hold.

If any condition above is missing, ambiguous, red, pending, dirty, or stale, fail
closed and follow the ordinary ship loop instead. A vague request to "hold" or
"review later" is never enough to create the PARKED state.

## Terminal quiescence (Sol commission #6379)

A terminal state — PARKED, or a ratified external `SHIP LOOP BLOCKED` ladder
exit — gets exactly ONE terminal report. After it, do not create new background
wake timers, do not re-poll, and when a leftover background task completes and
wakes the session, end that turn without re-reporting: the guard latches the
frozen state and passes the wake silently. At most one background ship-state
watcher may exist at a time while waiting on external machinery; a second
overlapping timer is refused — rely on the existing one. A hold release, a new
head, or any materially changed state clears the latch and the ordinary
fail-closed ship loop resumes; the latch never makes unfinished work easier to
abandon.
